Overview

Today I practiced curl and wget commands in Ubuntu Linux.

I learned how to check websites, download files, measure response time, and test APIs.

=> What I Practiced

1. Check Website Response
curl -I https://www.google.com

This command displays the HTTP headers of a website.

I learned how to check whether a website is responding.

2. Download a File Using curl
curl -o robots.txt https://www.google.com/robots.txt

This command downloads a file and saves it with the name robots.txt.

3. Download a File Using wget
wget -O robots-wget.txt https://www.google.com/robots.txt

This command downloads a file and saves it with a specific filenam

4. Check the Downloaded File
ls -lh robots.txt
cat robots.txt

I learned how to check the file size and view its contents.

5. Check Website Response Time
curl -o /dev/null -s -w "Time: %{time_total}s\n" https://www.google.com

This command measures how long the website request takes.

I learned how to perform a basic website response-time check.

6. Test an API
curl https://api.github.com

This command sends a request to the GitHub API and displays the response.

=> As a Linux Support Engineer, these commands are useful for:

Checking whether a website is responding.
Downloading files from a web server.
Checking basic HTTP connectivity.
Measuring website response time.
Testing APIs.

Note:-

1. STATUS=$(command)

This means:

"Run the command inside $(...), take its output, and store it in STATUS."

=> In our script:

STATUS=$(curl ...)

The output of curl becomes the value of STATUS.

2. -s → Silent mode

This means:

"Do not display the normal curl progress or error messages."

=> Why use it in a script?
Because we only want the status code, not unnecessary output.

3. -o /dev/null → Throw away the website content

Here:

-o = Save output to a file.
/dev/null = A special Linux location that discards anything written to it.

4. -w → Write a custom result

This means:

"After the request, print the information I specify."
